/-!
Aeneas writes the two options above at the top of each generated file. Each
sets a limit at which Lean stops with an error instead of continuing.

* `maxHeartbeats` limits the work that Lean may do on a single command, such as
  a definition or a theorem. The work is measured in thousands of small memory
  allocations, called heartbeats, so the limit does not depend on the speed of
  the computer. The default is `200000`, and `0` removes the limit. A command
  that exceeds it fails with the error `(deterministic) timeout`.
* `maxRecDepth` limits the depth of nested recursive calls in many procedures
  of Lean, such as elaboration and `simp`. The default is `512`. A command that
  exceeds it fails with the error `maximum recursion depth has been reached`.

Aeneas raises the two limits to `1000000` and `2048`, since large generated
definitions can exceed the defaults. The comments above the options name the
command-line options of Aeneas that change these values.
-/

/-!
# Addition with overflow

The Rust function

```rust
pub fn add_ten(x: u32) -> u32 {
    x + 10
}
```

adds `10` to an unsigned 32-bit integer. The type `u32` has the values
`0, 1, …, 2 ^ 32 - 1`. When `x + 10` exceeds `2 ^ 32 - 1`, the addition
**overflows**. By default, a Rust program compiled in debug mode then panics,
and a program compiled in release mode computes the sum modulo `2 ^ 32`. Aeneas
translates an arithmetic operation that can overflow into an operation that
fails on overflow.

The definitions above the comment `Specs & Formal verification` were generated
by Aeneas from `add_ten/src/lib.rs`. The specifications and proofs below it
are not part of the translation.

