## Specifications

A specification of a function that returns a `Result` describes its result. The
notation

  `f x ⦃ res => P res ⦄`

states that `f x` is `ok res` for some `res` with `P res`. In the braces, `res`
is a name for the result of `f x`, and `P res`, a proposition about this result,
is the **postcondition**. In `add_ten.spec` below, the braces contain
`res => res.val = x.val + 10`. As with `fun res => …`, the name `res` is bound,
and any other name can be used instead.

The notation stands for `WP.spec (f x) (fun res => P res)`, where `WP` is a
namespace of the Aeneas library. The name stands for **weakest precondition**, a
notion of program verification introduced by Dijkstra in 1975. It is a
condition on the input that holds exactly when the program terminates with a
result that satisfies the postcondition. The proposition `WP.spec (ok v) P` is
